That is the point at which risk becomes opportunity cost. The same destination, employment, appears in both cases, but with different meanings: $\begin{aligned}
\text{try, fail} &\longrightarrow \text{job as fallback}, \\
\text{do not try} &\longrightarrow \text{job as regret site}.\end{aligned}$ This is why the speaker’s answer belongs in a chapter on entrepreneurial judgment rather than a chapter on boldness. The claim is not that risk is automatically good. The claim is that a founder must compare the true failure path with the true inaction path.

